1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,836
Aarav clicks a photo.

2
00:00:01,836 --> 00:00:03,970
The camera flash fires in a blink.

3
00:00:04,270 --> 00:00:05,448
He clicks again.

4
00:00:05,448 --> 00:00:06,348
Nothing.

5
00:00:06,128 --> 00:00:08,005
The flash is not ready yet.

6
00:00:08,305 --> 00:00:10,894
Why does the flash make
him wait a few seconds?

7
00:00:11,394 --> 00:00:15,255
Think of a water tank, filled
from a tap through a thin pipe.

8
00:00:15,605 --> 00:00:18,004
At first, the water rushes in fast.

9
00:00:18,354 --> 00:00:21,677
As the tank fills, the
water inside pushes back.

10
00:00:22,027 --> 00:00:23,987
So the flow gets slower and slower.

11
00:00:24,337 --> 00:00:26,781
A circuit can do exactly the same thing.

12
00:00:27,131 --> 00:00:28,940
The tank is a capacitor.

13
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:30,790
It stores electric charge.

14
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,082
The thin pipe is a resistor.

15
00:00:33,082 --> 00:00:35,023
It slows the flow of charge.

16
00:00:35,373 --> 00:00:39,185
A resistor and a capacitor
together make an RC circuit.

17
00:00:39,835 --> 00:00:41,872
Let's build one on the breadboard.

18
00:00:42,222 --> 00:00:46,944
A ten volt battery, a resistor,
a capacitor and a two way switch.

19
00:00:47,294 --> 00:00:48,884
Flip the switch to charge.

20
00:00:48,884 --> 00:00:50,336
Electrons start to flow.

21
00:00:50,686 --> 00:00:51,847
Watch the scope.

22
00:00:51,847 --> 00:00:54,708
The capacitor voltage
shoots up fast at first.

23
00:00:55,058 --> 00:00:57,905
Then it slows down, and
creeps up towards ten volts.

24
00:00:58,255 --> 00:01:00,178
The current does the opposite.

25
00:01:00,178 --> 00:01:02,251
It starts big, then dies away.

26
00:01:02,601 --> 00:01:05,663
When the capacitor is
full, the current stops.

27
00:01:06,313 --> 00:01:08,408
Now flip the switch the other way.

28
00:01:08,408 --> 00:01:10,371
The battery is out of the loop.

29
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:14,314
The capacitor pushes its charge
back out through the resistor.

30
00:01:14,664 --> 00:01:19,091
Its voltage falls fast, then
slower and slower, down to zero.

31
00:01:19,741 --> 00:01:21,734
But why does the filling slow down?

32
00:01:22,084 --> 00:01:25,835
The battery's ten volts are shared
by the resistor and the capacitor.

33
00:01:26,185 --> 00:01:28,608
At the start, the capacitor is empty.

34
00:01:28,608 --> 00:01:30,652
The resistor gets all ten volts.

35
00:01:31,002 --> 00:01:32,514
So the current is big.

36
00:01:32,864 --> 00:01:36,337
As charge piles up, the
capacitor voltage rises.

37
00:01:36,337 --> 00:01:37,605
It pushes back.

38
00:01:37,955 --> 00:01:40,397
Less voltage is left for the resistor.

39
00:01:40,397 --> 00:01:41,955
So the current shrinks.

40
00:01:42,305 --> 00:01:44,351
Less current means slower filling.

41
00:01:44,351 --> 00:01:46,178
That is why the curve bends.

42
00:01:46,828 --> 00:01:48,533
So how fast does it fill?

43
00:01:48,533 --> 00:01:49,989
One number decides it.

44
00:01:50,339 --> 00:01:53,766
Multiply the resistance
R by the capacitance C.

45
00:01:54,466 --> 00:01:56,311
This is the time constant.

46
00:01:56,311 --> 00:01:58,884
We write it as tau, a Greek letter.

47
00:01:59,584 --> 00:02:03,711
After one tau, the capacitor is
about sixty three percent full.

48
00:02:04,411 --> 00:02:07,040
After two tau, about eighty six percent.

49
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:10,376
After five tau, more
than ninety nine percent.

50
00:02:10,376 --> 00:02:11,702
We call that full.

51
00:02:12,402 --> 00:02:15,283
Bigger R or bigger C means a bigger tau.

52
00:02:15,283 --> 00:02:16,925
The tank fills slower.

53
00:02:17,575 --> 00:02:19,636
Now the rules, step by step.

54
00:02:19,636 --> 00:02:20,730
Charging first.

55
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:25,538
VC equals V, times one minus
e to the minus t over tau.

56
00:02:26,238 --> 00:02:28,972
Here, e is not the charge of an electron.

57
00:02:29,322 --> 00:02:32,829
It is a special number, about
two point seven one eight.

58
00:02:33,529 --> 00:02:35,077
Put t equal to tau.

59
00:02:35,077 --> 00:02:37,265
You get one minus one over e.

60
00:02:37,615 --> 00:02:39,972
That is zero point six three two.

61
00:02:39,972 --> 00:02:41,425
Sixty three percent.

62
00:02:42,125 --> 00:02:46,124
The current is I nought, times
e to the minus t over tau.

63
00:02:46,824 --> 00:02:50,451
I nought is V over R, the
current at the very start.

64
00:02:51,151 --> 00:02:55,896
Discharging: VC equals V nought,
times e to the minus t over tau.

65
00:02:56,596 --> 00:03:00,859
After one tau, only thirty seven
percent of the voltage is left.

66
00:03:01,559 --> 00:03:03,231
Why is tau in seconds?

67
00:03:03,231 --> 00:03:05,329
An ohm is a volt per ampere.

68
00:03:05,679 --> 00:03:07,842
A farad is a coulomb per volt.

69
00:03:08,542 --> 00:03:09,613
Multiply them.

70
00:03:09,613 --> 00:03:10,894
The volts cancel.

71
00:03:10,894 --> 00:03:13,315
A coulomb per ampere is one second.

72
00:03:13,965 --> 00:03:15,849
R times C sets the pace.

73
00:03:15,849 --> 00:03:18,358
One tau, sixty three percent full.

74
00:03:19,158 --> 00:03:20,302
Worked example.

75
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:22,663
Our breadboard used ten kilo ohms.

76
00:03:23,013 --> 00:03:24,826
And two hundred and twenty microfarads.

77
00:03:25,176 --> 00:03:26,632
Tau equals R times C.

78
00:03:26,632 --> 00:03:29,473
Ten thousand times two
hundred twenty millionths.

79
00:03:29,823 --> 00:03:31,567
That is two point two seconds.

80
00:03:32,217 --> 00:03:36,420
After one tau, the voltage is zero
point six three two times ten.

81
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:38,905
That is about six point three volts.

82
00:03:39,555 --> 00:03:41,455
Full takes about five tau.

83
00:03:41,455 --> 00:03:44,413
Five times two point
two is eleven seconds.

84
00:03:45,063 --> 00:03:46,909
The flash works like this.

85
00:03:46,909 --> 00:03:49,634
Its capacitor needs a few tau to refill.

86
00:03:50,284 --> 00:03:51,748
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:52,098 --> 00:03:55,229
Many students think the
capacitor is full after one tau.

88
00:03:55,579 --> 00:03:56,479
It is not.

89
00:03:56,467 --> 00:03:59,808
After one tau, it is only
sixty three percent full.

90
00:04:00,158 --> 00:04:01,992
Full takes about five tau.

91
00:04:02,642 --> 00:04:04,020
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:05,397
In PUC board papers.

93
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:08,802
And in JEE Main, for example
in April twenty eighteen.

94
00:04:09,152 --> 00:04:13,944
Find tau, the voltage after some
time, or the time to reach a voltage.

95
00:04:14,594 --> 00:04:15,494
Try this.

96
00:04:15,432 --> 00:04:19,991
A four microfarad capacitor at twelve
volts discharges through five hundred

97
00:04:19,991 --> 00:04:20,891
kilo ohms.

98
00:04:20,881 --> 00:04:23,089
When does it reach three volts?

99
00:04:23,089 --> 00:04:26,993
Take the natural log of two
as zero point six nine three.

100
00:04:32,393 --> 00:04:33,293
C.

101
00:04:32,784 --> 00:04:35,079
About two point seven seven seconds.

102
00:04:35,429 --> 00:04:37,035
First, tau equals RC.

103
00:04:37,035 --> 00:04:40,469
Five hundred thousand times
four millionths is two seconds.

104
00:04:40,819 --> 00:04:43,746
Twelve volts down to three
volts is one quarter.

105
00:04:43,746 --> 00:04:45,159
That is two halvings.

106
00:04:45,509 --> 00:04:48,836
Each halving takes tau times
the natural log of two.

107
00:04:48,836 --> 00:04:51,111
About one point three nine seconds.

108
00:04:51,461 --> 00:04:54,503
So two halvings take about
two point seven seven seconds.

109
00:04:55,153 --> 00:04:56,700
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:01,184
Next, RL circuits.

111
00:05:01,184 --> 00:05:04,403
What happens when a coil fights
a sudden change in current?
